  "fullText": "A **Git branch** is a lightweight pointer to a sequence of commits inside a single Git repository.  \n- You create it locally (or on a shared repo) to develop a feature or fix a bug.  \n- All collaborators work on the same repository; branches are merged back into the main branch (often `main`/`master`) when finished.\n\nA **GitHub fork** is a *full copy* of an entire repository, hosted in a separate GitHub account.  \n- It includes every branch and commit from the original repository.  \n- It is useful when you don’t have write access to the original repo or want to maintain a completely isolated development history.  \n- You usually push your changes to branches inside your fork and then submit a pull request to merge them back into the upstream repo.\n\nIn short: a branch is an internal branch inside a repo; a fork is a separate clone of the whole repo on GitHub, often used for contributing to projects you don’t own.",
